course: dictionary
lesson: 1746
title: Holding on: the word clasp
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Clasp is a word with two jobs: it can be a verb, an action, or a noun, a thing. When you meet it, read the words around it to find out which meaning fits. «He clasps the crag with crooked hands.» An eagle sits on a rock and holds on tightly, so clasps must mean holds tightly.
explain: Tennyson's poem gives the eagle hands, but eagles have claws, not hands. This is personification, where a thing is described as if it were a person. The hard c sound in clasps, crag and crooked is alliteration, which makes the line sound strong and firm.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «He holds the crag with crooked hands» still makes sense, so holds is a good match for clasps. In everyday life a clasp is the small fastener on a necklace, bag or belt, which holds two parts together.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Clasp» as a verb means to hold something tightly, as in «She clasped her teddy».
fact: «Clasp» as a verb can also mean to put your hands together with the fingers locked, as in «clasp your hands».
fact: «Clasp» as a noun is a small metal fastener that holds two parts together, such as on a necklace, bag or belt.
fact: «Clasp» as a noun can also mean a tight hold or grip, as in «a firm clasp».
exercise: «She clasped her teddy tightly all night.» What does clasped mean? | Looked at carefully | *Held tightly | Lost somewhere | Dropped on the floor
exercise: On a necklace, what is the clasp? | *A small metal fastener | A long chain of beads | A tiny bell | A bright stone
exercise: In «He clasps the crag with crooked hands», which word gives the eagle a human body part? | crag | with | *hands | clasps
test: In «a firm clasp», what does clasp mean? | A sweet taste | *A tight hold or grip | A loud noise | A long walk
test: What does clasp mean in «clasp your hands»? | *Put your hands together with the fingers locked | Throw something far | Wave your hands in the air | Wash your hands
exam: Tennyson writes «He clasps the crag with crooked hands» although eagles have no hands. What is this called? | Rhyme | Alliteration | *Personification | Onomatopoeia
